<div id="login">
  <span id="use-password"></span>
  {{{card}}}
  <a href="#use-password">Use a password</a>

  {{> js_setup nodes=nodes}}
  {{#if extraPartial }}
    {{> (extraPartial) }}
  {{/if}}
</div>
{{! The image's own login.hbs with three differences: two of the card's links
    are hidden, its E-Mail and password fields are closed until asked for,
    and its GitHub button is filled. The element and the link above are the
    second, and the rest is the rules below, each under its reason.

    READ AGAINST THE IMAGE
    sha256:46a7bac1ad0c67c692b37d0380613fd4618fd751afac2c5314aa8d3ae77ef1f0
    because the rules are about where that image draws things. One that drew
    the GitHub button in the password's form would have the closed card hide
    both, and leave a person new here only the link to a password. So
    tests/ory-ui-view.py holds ory-ui.yaml to that image, and another is named
    here once its card has been looked at under these rules. }}
<style>
  {{! The card links sign-up and recovery whether or not kratos.yaml enables
      them. It enables no recovery. It opens registration only to a person who
      signs in with GitHub from a browser holding an invite link, and that is
      the GitHub button on this card. The sign-up link starts a registration by
      hand instead, which kratos.yaml sends to a page of the console that
      registers nobody, so the link stays hidden. }}
  div:has(> a[data-testid="forgot-password-link"]),
  div:has(> div > a[data-testid="signup-link"]) {
    display: none;
  }

  {{! THE CARD OPENS ON GITHUB ALONE. A person new here has no password and
      cannot make one on this card, for the reasons above, and the image draws
      the password form open all the same, which reads as an account to be
      made. An identity that holds a password still signs in with it, so the
      form stays in the card, closed, and the link under the GitHub button
      opens it.

      NO SCRIPT. The link names the empty element before the card, and the
      form is closed only while the page's address does not. Following the
      link also moves where the Tab key starts to that element, so the keys
      after it walk the card from its top.

      A CARD THAT CARRIES A MESSAGE IS DRAWN OPEN. A refused password comes
      back as the same flow with a message, at an address without the
      fragment, and a closed form would hide what the message is about. A
      refresh carries one too, so it is drawn as the image draws it.

      BUT NOT BY THE MESSAGE THAT ITS FLOW EXPIRED. Kratos answers either
      button of a card left past its flow's lifetime with a new flow carrying
      that message, and a person new here who pressed the GitHub button would
      come back to a password form. So the message is drawn and the card stays
      closed. A person who had the form open comes back to it closed too, and
      asks again: a form's post keeps no fragment and the new flow nothing
      that was typed, so the page cannot tell the two people apart.

      NOR ON A FLOW THAT HAS BEEN SENT TO GITHUB, whatever message it carries.
      A person new here who pressed the GitHub button and pressed Cancel there
      comes back to the same flow under a message, and would be drawn a
      password form. So the message is drawn and the card stays closed. A
      password posted to such a flow leaves it a flow sent to GitHub, so a
      person who holds one and gets it wrong there comes back to a closed card
      as well, that refusal drawn over it, and asks once more; the E-Mail that
      was typed is kept.

      SUCH A FLOW IS KNOWN BY ITS CARD. The image draws the card's E-Mail
      field a second time on it, in the GitHub form above the button. The
      button signs in without it, and to a person new here it is something to
      fill in, so it is hidden: a person who pressed the button, did not
      finish at GitHub and opens the same address again meets the card they
      first met. A refresh keeps the identity's address in that form as well,
      in a field of the hidden type, and is no flow sent to GitHub; so the
      card is asked for a field of another type.

      ONLY A CARD THAT OFFERS BOTH IS CLOSED. Without the GitHub button a
      closed card would offer nothing but the link that opens it, and two
      cards come without it: a refresh for an identity that holds no GitHub
      account, and the one that asks for an identity's password before a
      GitHub account is added to it.

      The fields are read by the names Kratos gives them, which a form has to
      post under whatever draws it. The message is read by the image's test
      id, and so is the GitHub form where the card is asked which form holds
      an E-Mail field, because one :has() cannot hold another. Another image
      may carry neither id. Without the message's a refused password is still
      told, above a form that is closed; without the form's a card sent to
      GitHub is drawn open under its message, as the image draws it. }}
  #login {
    position: relative;
  }
  a[href="#use-password"] {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    bottom: 1.75rem;
    width: fit-content;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
    font-family: var(--ory-theme-font-family);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-muted);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }
  a[href="#use-password"]:hover {
    color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-def);
  }
  #use-password:not(:target)
    ~ div:has(button[name="provider"]):has(input[name="password"]):is(
      :not(
        :has(
          [data-testid^="ui/message/"]:not([data-testid="ui/message/4010001"])
        )
      ),
      :has(
        [data-testid="login-flow-oidc"]
          input[name="identifier"]:not([type="hidden"])
      )
    ) {
    {{! The room under the GitHub button that the link is drawn in. }}
    padding-bottom: 5.25rem;
    & form:has(input[name="password"]),
    & form:has(button[name="provider"]) + hr {
      display: none;
    }
    & ~ a[href="#use-password"] {
      display: block;
    }
  }
  {{! The E-Mail field of a flow that has been sent to GitHub. }}
  #login
    form:has(button[name="provider"])
    div:has(input[name="identifier"]):not(:has(button[name="provider"])) {
    display: none;
  }

  {{! The image draws the GitHub button outlined and the password's filled.
      This one is filled in the colours the image gives it while it is
      pressed. The image marks focus on it by the colour of its border, which
      a filled button does not show, so a ring is drawn: around the link, and
      inside the button, because the card's grids clip what is drawn outside
      one. }}
  #login button[name="provider"] {
    color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-on-dark);
    background-color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-def);
    border-color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-def);
  }
  #login button[name="provider"]:hover,
  #login button[name="provider"]:active {
    background-color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-muted);
    border-color: var(--ory-theme-foreground-muted);
  }
  #login button[name="provider"]:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--ory-theme-foreground-on-dark);
    outline-offset: -5px;
  }
  a[href="#use-password"]:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--ory-theme-accent-def);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
</style>
